  {
    "title": "Claude Skills Are Just Files the Model Loads",
    "topic_source": "seed",
    "pillar": "assistant-features",
    "hook": "A Skill is not a plugin. It is a folder.",
    "takeaway": "Capability is context, delivered at the right moment.",
    "caption": "A Skill is not a plugin. It is a folder.\nHere is what actually happens when a model 'gains a skill':\n1. Registry — the tool lists each skill by name and one-line description only.\n2. Match — the model picks a skill from that description, like choosing a tool.\n3. Load — only then does the full instruction file enter the context window.\n4. Follow — the model executes those instructions instead of its default behavior.\nNothing is fine-tuned and no weights change. You are paying for context discipline: the heavy instructions stay out of the window until they are needed.\n📌 Save this before you cram everything into one system prompt.\n🔁 Send it to someone who thinks skills are magic.\nWritten by Kai.\nFollow @byteflowlabs for AI systems, no hype.",
    "hashtags": [
      "#claudeai",
      "#llm",
      "#aiengineering",
      "#promptengineering"
    ],
    "footage_queries": [
      "library archive shelves",
      "circuit board macro",
      "data center corridor",
      "slow abstract particles"
    ],
    "scenes": [
      {
        "layout": "vertical-stack",
        "heading": "Skill = deferred context",
        "nodes": [
          {
            "id": "req",
            "icon": "💬",
            "label": "REQUEST"
          },
          {
            "id": "reg",
            "label": "SKILL LIST"
          },
          {
            "id": "file",
            "icon": "📄",
            "label": "SKILL FILE"
          },
          {
            "id": "ctx",
            "icon": "🧠",
            "label": "CONTEXT"
          }
        ],
        "steps": [
          {
            "from": "req",
            "to": "reg",
            "packet": "MATCH",
            "color": "accent",
            "status": "name and one line, nothing more"
          },
          {
            "from": "reg",
            "to": "file",
            "packet": "LOAD",
            "color": "accent",
            "status": "only the chosen file is read"
          },
          {
            "from": "file",
            "to": "ctx",
            "packet": "RULES",
            "color": "good",
            "status": "instructions enter the window"
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "title": "Why ChatGPT Forgets What You Said 30 Turns Ago",
    "topic_source": "seed",
    "pillar": "model-internals",
    "hook": "It never forgot. It never saw it.",
    "takeaway": "Memory is a re-send, not a recollection.",
    "caption": "It never forgot. It never saw it.\nHere is what actually happens on a long chat:\n1. Stateless — the model has no memory between messages at all.\n2. Re-send — the app pastes the whole conversation back in on every turn.\n3. Budget — that transcript competes with your new question for the same token budget.\n4. Trim — when it no longer fits, the oldest turns are dropped or summarized.\n5. Gap — the model answers confidently from what remains, so it looks like forgetting.\nSeparate 'memory' features are just a stored note re-injected into the same window.\n📌 Save this before you blame the model for forgetting.\n🔁 Send it to someone with a 200-message thread.\nWritten by Kai.\nFollow @byteflowlabs for AI systems, no hype.",
    "hashtags": [
      "#chatgpt",
      "#llm",
      "#context",
      "#aiengineering"
    ],
    "footage_queries": [
      "old paper archive",
      "server room walkthrough",
      "fiber optic cables closeup",
      "quiet night city timelapse"
    ],
    "scenes": [
